<p>
A short test of your intuition: what&rsquo;s the most recognized English word in the world? There are, of course, a bunch of words that everybody know in every language: please, thank you, bathroom&hellip; but this is an English word that everyone knows. Give up? <a href="https://www.merriam-webster.com/wordplay/the-hilarious-history-of-ok-okay">The word is &ldquo;OK&rdquo;</a>. I&rsquo;m always sort of surprised when I see it in non-English text but most languages have made it their own.
</p>
<p>
That&rsquo;s pretty amazing but the most amazing thing about the word is that it was originally a joke. Although many of us might consider the hacker love of word play a relatively modern phenomenon, it&rsquo;s actually a long standing tradition, at least in English. The story of that joke is a bit long and complicated so you should follow the above link to discover all the details, including why it survived in the language while similar locutions did not.
</p>
<p>
Finally, despite what your fifth grade English teacher, Miss Frump, may have told you, the correct spelling is &ldquo;OK&rdquo;, not &ldquo;okay&rdquo; and not &ldquo;ok&rdquo;. That will make sense to you when you discover its origin.
</p>
<p>
I love this kind of thing but it is, after all, a well established aspect of the hacker culture. If nothing else, you can astound your parents and normie friends with this piece of arcana.</p>
